Subject: [PATCH] fonts: retry Windows missing-family stretch suffixes on Linux

Linux's FCI matching rejects an Arial face returned for Arial Narrow while
Arial Narrow remains the first post-config family. A Fontconfig accept list
can therefore look correct under fc-match while Blink advances to the next
CSS fallback family. Windows instead retries an unavailable family name with
its recognized stretch suffix removed and the matching stretch requested.

Use that existing native policy for Windows profiles on Linux. A matching
original face wins. The retry copies the requested description and changes
only the stretch as specified by the suffix. The resulting font remains a
real matched face; no synthetic horizontal scaling is added. Local unique
names, explicit file/TTC lookup and last-resort matching bypass this policy.
Non-Windows profiles and non-suffix family handling remain unchanged.

Primary source at Chromium79460ebecaa5625e57a5fb679a735659e73dc687:
- platform/fonts/win/font_cache_skia_win.cc:302-329,371-403,427-481
- platform/fonts/skia/font_cache_skia.cc:262-295,299-355
Paths above are under third_party/blink/renderer/.
Skia0873ec164a06966b90ae0d43ef783cfb180084ae:
- src/ports/SkFontConfigInterface_direct.cpp:552-586,623-672

The Arial Narrow control now exists, and it confirms this branch.
resources/fingerprints/raw/windows-chrome-20260910T140813Z.json measures
40px "Arial Narrow" at width 160.05859375 with fontBoundingBox ascent 36 and
descent 8, which is byte-identical to 40px Arial in the same capture. So the
reference Windows host has no narrow face, took exactly this retry, and got
Arial back at its normal width with no synthetic condensing -- which is why this
patch adds none.

The same capture's detection probe is the sharper half of the control. It
reports "Arial Narrow" as detected, measuring 720 against all three generic
baselines, the same triple Arial produces, while families that are genuinely
absent and carry no stretch suffix -- Menlo and Zapfino -- return the baseline
triple 587/720/692 and are not detected. Without this retry a Windows persona on
Linux advances to the next CSS fallback family, returns the baseline triple, and
is missing from its detected set a family every real Windows machine reports.
That is the divergence the patch removes, and it is readable by any detector
that probes font presence by width.

What the control does not settle: on a host that does have a real narrow face
installed, Fontconfig returns it for the condensed request and the advances
differ from the reference, where Windows had nothing narrower to hand back. That
is an outcome of which font files the host carries, not a question about this
policy, and it resolves the same way every other font question does -- the user
installs the claimed platform's set.

V1 compilation and V3 checks remain required, including a real narrow family,
style/stretch requests and src:local() exclusion. No font files, fontconfig,
profile schema, JavaScript API, or Skia matching allowlist is modified.
diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/skia/font_cache_skia.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/skia/font_cache_skia.cc
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/skia/font_cache_skia.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/skia/font_cache_skia.cc
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
 #include <memory>
 #include <utility>
 
+#include "base/apostate/profile.h"
 #include "base/check_op.h"
 #include "base/metrics/histogram_functions.h"
 #include "base/notreached.h"
@@ -70,6 +71,59 @@
 }
 
 namespace {
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+bool MatchesWindowsFamily(SkTypeface* face, const AtomicString& family) {
+  auto* names = face->createFamilyNameIterator();
+  SkTypeface::LocalizedString name;
+  bool matches = false;
+  while (names->next(&name)) {
+    if (DeprecatedEqualIgnoringCase(family, ToAtomicString(name.fString))) {
+      matches = true;
+      break;
+    }
+  }
+  names->unref();
+  if (!matches) {
+    SkString primary;
+    face->getFamilyName(&primary);
+    matches = DeprecatedEqualIgnoringCase(family, ToAtomicString(primary));
+  }
+  return matches;
+}
+
+bool WindowsStretchSuffix(const AtomicString& family,
+                          AtomicString& adjusted_name,
+                          FontSelectionValue& stretch) {
+  // Same backward-compatible family-name translation as
+  // win/font_cache_skia_win.cc. This changes a font request, not glyph scale.
+  struct Suffix {
+    const char* text;
+    wtf_size_t length;
+    FontSelectionValue stretch;
+  };
+  static const Suffix kSuffixes[] = {
+      {" ultracondensed", 15, kUltraCondensedWidthValue},
+      {" extracondensed", 15, kExtraCondensedWidthValue},
+      {" condensed", 10, kCondensedWidthValue},
+      {" narrow", 7, kCondensedWidthValue},
+      {" semicondensed", 14, kSemiCondensedWidthValue},
+      {" semiexpanded", 13, kSemiExpandedWidthValue},
+      {" expanded", 9, kExpandedWidthValue},
+      {" extraexpanded", 14, kExtraExpandedWidthValue},
+      {" ultraexpanded", 14, kUltraExpandedWidthValue}};
+  for (const auto& suffix : kSuffixes) {
+    if (family.EndsWithIgnoringAsciiCase(suffix.text)) {
+      StringView base_family(family);
+      base_family.remove_suffix(suffix.length);
+      adjusted_name = AtomicString(base_family);
+      stretch = suffix.stretch;
+      return true;
+    }
+  }
+  return false;
+}
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+
 const FontPlatformData* CreateFontPlatformDataForTypeface(
     sk_sp<SkTypeface> typeface,
     const FontDescription& font_description) {
@@ -327,6 +381,29 @@
   typeface = CreateTypeface(font_description, creation_params, name);
 #endif
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+  const auto* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get();
+  if (profile && profile->ua_platform() &&
+      *profile->ua_platform() == "Windows" &&
+      creation_params.CreationType() == kCreateFontByFamily &&
+      alternate_name != AlternateFontName::kLocalUniqueFace &&
+      alternate_name != AlternateFontName::kLastResort &&
+      (!typeface ||
+       !MatchesWindowsFamily(typeface.get(), creation_params.Family()))) {
+    AtomicString adjusted_name;
+    FontSelectionValue stretch;
+    if (WindowsStretchSuffix(creation_params.Family(), adjusted_name, stretch)) {
+      FontDescription adjusted_description = font_description;
+      adjusted_description.SetStretch(stretch);
+      typeface = CreateTypeface(adjusted_description,
+                                FontFaceCreationParams(adjusted_name), name);
+      if (!typeface || !MatchesWindowsFamily(typeface.get(), adjusted_name)) {
+        return nullptr;
+      }
+    }
+  }
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+
   if (!typeface)
     return nullptr;
 
